<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Imru al-Qays ibn Amr</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 328</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Imru al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qays">Qays</a> ibn Amr was an Arab king of the early 4th century, usually counted as an early Lakhmid ruler of al-Hira. His epitaph, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namara-inscription">Namara inscription</a> of 328 found in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, calls him king of all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> and is one of the earliest texts in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabic-language">Arabic language</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/imru-al-qays-ibn-amr.jpg" alt="Imru al-Qays ibn Amr" width="320" height="113" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marie-Lan Nguyen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Epitaph_Imru-l-Qays_Louvre_AO4083.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
